    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Raised among the Hawaiian nobility and schooled by American missionaries, Liliʻuokalani became heir apparent in 1877 and queen in 1891, the only woman to reign over the Hawaiian Kingdom.</li><li>Her attempt to proclaim a constitution restoring the powers of the monarchy and the votes of disenfranchised Hawaiians and Asians led a Committee of Safety, backed by the landing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces, to depose her in January 1893.</li><li>Imprisoned and made to abdicate in 1895, she spent years protesting annexation and seeking the return of the Crown Lands, and is remembered also as a composer and writer.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Following Hawaiian custom, she was named after an event at her birth: the regent Elizabeth Kīnaʻu had an eye infection, and gave the child names meaning smarting, tearful, a burning pain and sore eyes. The name Liliʻuokalani, &quot;the smarting of the royal ones&quot;, dates from 1877.</p></aside>
    </section>

