    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1838</span><span class="rf-text">Lydia Liliʻu Loloku Walania Kamakaʻeha is born on 2 September in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1862</span><span class="rf-text">She marries the American-born John Owen Dominis on 16 September and moves into Washington Place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1877</span><span class="rf-text">After the death of her brother Leleiohoku she is designated heir apparent on 10 April and given the name Liliʻuokalani by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1891</span><span class="rf-text">She takes the oath of office on 29 January, nine days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a>'s death, as the first and only female monarch of the Hawaiian Kingdom</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1893</span><span class="rf-text">She is deposed on 17 January and a provisional government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanford-b-dole">Sanford B. Dole</a> is recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-l-stevens">John L. Stevens</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1895</span><span class="rf-text">Arrested after a failed royalist rebellion, she signs a document of abdication on 24 January in return for the release of her jailed supporters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text">Hawaiʻi is annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newlands-resolution">Newlands Resolution</a>; she boycotts the ceremony of 12 August and publishes her memoir</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">She dies on 11 November at Washington Place and receives a state funeral in the throne room of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii.jpg" alt="Kamehameha III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photo_of_Kamehameha_III_(PP-97-7-003).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hugo Stangenwald, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liliʻuokalani was born Lydia Liliʻu Loloku Walania Kamakaʻeha on 2 September 1838, in the grass house of her maternal grandfather ʻAikanaka at the base of Punchbowl Crater in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>. Her parents, Analea Keohokālole and Caesar Kapaʻakea, were of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a> class of the Hawaiian nobility, collateral relations of the reigning House of Kamehameha and descendants of two of the five royal counsellors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>. She was the third surviving child of a large family that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">David Kalākaua</a>, Miriam Likelike and William Pitt Leleiohoku II. By the custom of hānai, an informal adoption within extended families, she was given at birth to Abner Pākī and his wife Laura Kōnia and raised with their daughter Bernice Pauahi.</p><p>In 1842, aged four, she entered the Chiefs' Children's School, later the Royal School, whose pupils <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> had formally proclaimed eligible for the throne. The American missionaries Amos Starr Cooke and Juliette Montague Cooke taught her and her royal cousins in English. She later looked back on those years unfavourably, remembering being "sent hungry to bed" and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> epidemic of 1848 that killed a classmate and her younger sister Kaʻiminaʻauao. After the boarding school closed around 1850 she attended a day school and lived with her hānai parents at Haleʻākala. Pākī died in 1855 and Kōnia two years later, and she came under the guardianship of Bernice Pauahi and her American husband, Charles Reed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>. At the court of Kamehameha IV she was a close friend and attendant of Queen Emma.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalakaua.jpg" alt="Kalākaua" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdavidkalakaua_dust.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James J. Williams, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After a brief engagement to William Charles Lunalilo, which she broke off, she married the American-born John Owen Dominis, a secretary to Kamehameha IV, on 16 September 1862. The marriage was reportedly unhappy; the couple had no children of their own, and she adopted three hānai children. She kept her place at court and in 1866 composed "He Mele Lāhui Hawaiʻi" as a national anthem at the request of Kamehameha V. When Lunalilo, the first elected king, died without an heir in 1874, the legislature chose her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">David Kalākaua</a> over the dowager queen Emma, a result that caused a riot at the courthouse. Kalākaua gave his siblings royal titles and named their brother Leleiohoku heir.</p><p>Leleiohoku died in 1877, and on 10 April she was designated heir apparent; Kalākaua then changed her name to Liliʻuokalani. She served as regent during his world tour of 1881, when she met a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic by closing the ports and imposing a quarantine; there were 789 cases and 289 deaths, mainly among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Native Hawaiians</a>. She visited the Kalaupapa leper settlement, founded a savings bank for women and an educational society for Hawaiian girls in 1886, and in 1887 attended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s Golden Jubilee in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. There she learned that Kalākaua had been forced, under threat of death, to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a>. She was regent again from November 1890, when the king left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> on 20 January 1891; the news reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> on 29 January, and she took the oath of office that day.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liliʻuokalani began her reign by requiring the resignation of her brother's cabinet, a step the Supreme Court upheld with one dissent. On 9 March 1891 she named her niece Kaʻiulani as successor, and from April to July she toured the main islands. Her husband, made Prince Consort and restored as Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, died on 27 August 1891. From May 1892 to January 1893 the legislature sat for an unprecedented 171 days, divided among four parties; under the constitution of 1887 it could dismiss her ministers, and it voted out four of her cabinets.</p><p>From early in her reign she received petitions to rewrite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a>, a demand supported by two-thirds of the registered voters. With the legislators Joseph Nāwahī and William Pūnohu White she drafted a constitution that would have restored power to the monarchy and voting rights to economically disenfranchised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Native Hawaiians</a> and Asians. On 13 January 1893 she appointed a cabinet chosen to support its promulgation, but her ministers withheld their backing, and on 16 January she and her supporters abandoned the attempt to proclaim it unilaterally. She was deposed the next day. She yielded her authority temporarily to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, not to the provisional government, in the hope that Washington would restore Hawaiian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. She was also an author and songwriter: she wrote "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aloha-oe">Aloha ʻOe</a>", translated the creation chant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a>, published in 1897, and set out her view of her country's history in Hawaiʻi's Story by Hawaiʻi's Queen (1898).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lorrin-a-thurston"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorrin-a-thurston.jpg" alt="Lorrin A. Thurston" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lorrin-a-thurston">Lorrin A. Thurston</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lorrin_A._Thurston,_1892.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Her opponents were led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorrin-a-thurston">Lorrin A. Thurston</a>, a grandson of American missionaries, and William Owen Smith. Calling her constitutional plan "revolutionary", they formed a 13-member Committee of Safety that drew its support mainly from the American and European business class and aimed to depose the queen and seek annexation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Her own ministers and closest friends had opposed the plan and tried to dissuade her. When the Marshal of the Kingdom, Charles Burnett Wilson, asked for warrants to arrest the committee, the cabinet refused, fearing escalation. Marines and sailors from the USS <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> landed; they fired no shot, but their presence intimidated the royalist defenders. The historian William Russ states that "the injunction to prevent fighting of any kind made it impossible for the monarchy to protect itself".</p><p>American judgements were divided. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blount-report">Blount Report</a>, commissioned by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grover-cleveland">Grover Cleveland</a>, concluded that the overthrow was illegal and that the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-l-stevens">John L. Stevens</a> and American troops had acted inappropriately; the Senate's Morgan Report of 26 February 1894 found Stevens and all parties except the queen "not guilty". Cleveland offered to restore her if she granted an amnesty. Her first reply, that Hawaiian law prescribed confiscation of property and death for treason and that only her ministers could set it aside, lost her the goodwill of his administration, and when she changed her position the provisional government refused to reinstate her.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iolani-palace.jpg" alt="ʻIolani Palace" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iolani_Palace_(51872681413).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gage Skidmore from Surprise, AZ, United States of America, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The provisional government became the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> on 4 July 1894, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanford-b-dole">Sanford B. Dole</a> as president. In January 1895 Robert W. Wilcox and Samuel Nowlein led a rebellion to restore the monarchy. It failed, and on 16 January Liliʻuokalani was arrested and confined in an upstairs bedroom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a> after firearms were found at Washington Place. On 24 January she signed a document of abdication in return for the release of her jailed supporters, six of whom had been sentenced to hang; she wrote later that she would have chosen death for herself. A military commission tried her in the throne room on 8 February and sentenced her to five years of hard labour and a fine of $5,000, commuted on 4 September to imprisonment in the palace. On 13 October 1896 the Republic gave her a full pardon.</p><p>She then divided her time between Hawaii and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a> In 1897 she filed a formal protest against a new annexation treaty, which failed in the Senate after Native Hawaiian delegates presented petitions bearing over 21,000 signatures. Hawaii was annexed all the same by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newlands-resolution">Newlands Resolution</a> in July 1898, and she and her household stayed away from the ceremony of 12 August. For more than a decade she sought recompense for the Crown Lands seized by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; her lawsuit of 1909 failed, and in 1911 the Territory of Hawaii granted her a pension of $1,250 a month. She died there on the morning of 11 November 1917, aged seventy-nine, and after a state funeral on 18 November was entombed in the Royal Mausoleum at Mauna ʻAla.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liliʻuokalani's reign ended the Hawaiian monarchy, and the lands once reserved for it passed to the territorial government, the source of the "Ceded Lands" issue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. The historian Sydney Lehua Iaukea noted that the pension granted her in 1911 never addressed the legality of the seizure. By her will the Queen Liliʻuokalani Trust, established on 2 December 1909, received most of her estate for the care of orphaned and destitute children; lawsuits against it, one brought by Prince Jonah Kūhiō Kalanianaʻole in 1915, were resolved in 1923. Newspapers in Hawaii that had supported the overthrow acknowledged at her death the esteem in which she was held around the world.</p><p>Her music helped preserve traditional Hawaiian poetics while taking up the Western harmonies the missionaries had brought. "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aloha-oe">Aloha ʻOe</a>", written as a lover's farewell and transcribed during her confinement, came to be regarded as a lament for the loss of her country and is among the most recognisable Hawaiian songs; a collection, The Queen's Songbook, appeared in 1999. The Hawaiian Music Hall of Fame honours her and her siblings as Na Lani ʻEhā, "The Heavenly Four". An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outrigger-canoe">outrigger canoe</a> race, hula competitions and a festival in Hilo bear her name, and in 2001 the regents of the University of Hawaii described her as "an important link to traditional Hawaiian culture and society". On 19 January 2026 the Episcopal Church formally recognised her as a saint at her resting place, honouring her decision to avoid bloodshed during the overthrow.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Liliʻuokalani, born into the Hawaiian nobility in 1838, reigned as the last monarch of the Hawaiian Kingdom from 1891 to 1893. Her attempt to proclaim a new constitution brought her overthrow by annexationists backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces; she was later imprisoned, abdicated under duress and opposed annexation. She is remembered too as the composer of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aloha-oe">Aloha ʻOe</a>&quot;.</em></p>

